raisal clause","Property claim valuation","Hail damage","Ice dams","Snow load","Wind damage"],"openingHoursSpecification":[{"@type":"OpeningHoursSpecification","dayOfWeek":["Monday","Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday","Friday"],"opens":"08:00","closes":"18:00"}]},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/#website","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/","name":"Wisconsin Insurance Appraiser","publisher":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"inLanguage":"en-US"},{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#webpage","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/","name":"Wisconsin Insurance Appraisal Process | 8 Steps","description":"Step-by-step guide to the Wisconsin insurance appraisal process: the written demand, choosing appraisers and an umpire, and the binding award.","inLanguage":"en-US","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/#website"},"about":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/#business"},"dateModified":"2026-11-13","primaryImageOfPage":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp","width":1024,"height":680},"breadcrumb":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#breadcrumb"},"mainEntity":{"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#howto"}},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Appraisal process guide","item":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/"}],"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#breadcrumb"},{"@type":"HowTo","name":"How the insurance appraisal process works in Wisconsin","description":"Eight steps from a disputed estimate to a signed appraisal award.","step":[{"@type":"HowToStep","position":1,"name":"Pin down the disagreement","text":"Appraisal settles one kind of argument: how much a covered loss is worth. Before anyone sends a demand, get specific about where you and the carrier part ways. Is the gap in roof squares, in the price of siding, in whether drywall gets patched or replaced, or in overhead and profit? Lay your contrac","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-1"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":2,"name":"Read the appraisal provision","text":"Find the policy itself, not just the declarations page, and locate the paragraph usually titled “Appraisal” in the conditions section. Policies vary, so read your own wording rather than relying on a summary. Look for: Who may demand appraisal and whether the demand must be in writing. How many days","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-2"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":3,"name":"Send a written demand","text":"Once the gap is clear and the clause is understood, the demand itself is usually short. A typical demand letter: Identifies the policyholder, property address, policy number and claim number. States that the parties disagree on the amount of loss. Invokes the appraisal provision by name. Names your ","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-3"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":4,"name":"Choose your appraiser","text":"Your appraiser builds and defends your valuation, so choose someone who understands construction pricing, the type of damage involved and the appraisal procedure itself. Ask any candidate: How they’re paid, and whether the fee structure could be challenged under an impartiality requirement in your p","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-4"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":5,"name":"Select the umpire","text":"The two appraisers try to agree on a neutral umpire, ideally early, so one is in place before any impasse. A good umpire understands estimating and construction, has no ties to either side and is willing to look at evidence rather than split the difference by habit. If the appraisers can’t agree wit","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-5"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":6,"name":"Inspect and build estimates","text":"Each appraiser inspects the property, ideally with the other appraiser present, and documents what they see. For a roof that might mean measuring every slope, counting hail hits in test areas and noting the shingle line. For a water loss it might mean moisture readings, cut lines and contents lists.","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-6"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":7,"name":"Negotiate line by line","text":"With both estimates in hand, the appraisers compare them line by line. Many items settle quickly once measurements are reconciled or a missing component is spotted. The harder items, such as matching, repair versus replacement, or overhead and profit, take documentation and discussion. Agreed lines ","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-7"},{"@type":"HowToStep","position":8,"name":"Sign the award and get paid","text":"An award signed by the two appraisers, or by one appraiser and the umpire, generally sets the amount of loss. A clear award separates replacement cost from actual cash value and lists categories such as dwelling, other structures and contents, so payment can be calculated without new arguments. Afte","url":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#step-8"}],"@id":"https://wisconsininsuranceappraiser.com/appraisal-process-guide/#howto"}]}</script>
